From 02efacaceb507cd714eefe421d33d7cb28a07b00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 01:56:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] Stop the JSON stores losing a concurrent update

atomic_write_json has always guaranteed a reader never sees half a file. It
could not guarantee two writers do not overwrite each other, because the
damage is done before either write starts: both read, both mutate their own
copy, and the second os.replace wins. The docstring said so and stopped
there, calling locking "a separate, larger question".

Measured on this repository: twelve processes each queuing one publisher
action, and the store held **two**. Ten records gone. Nothing corrupt,
nothing anywhere saying so -- the same shape as the PIN-lockout defect fixed
earlier, minus the security consequence. And Dispatch runs the portal and
the launcher as two processes against one folder, so this is reachable
rather than theoretical.

store_lock() is an exclusive cross-process lock on one store, and guarded()
is the decorator that holds it across the whole read-modify-write. The
decorator was chosen over restructuring: every mutator already reads, edits
and saves, so one line above each of them fixes the race without touching
138 call sites and inventing 138 chances to get it wrong. It mirrors the
@atomic decorator the service layer already uses, so there is one idiom for
this in the codebase rather than two.

Applied to all forty-one mutating functions across all eleven stores.

The lock file sits beside the store rather than being the store. Two
reasons: os.replace swaps the file on every write, so a lock held on its
inode stops describing the file that is there; and a store that does not
exist yet still needs a lock, because the first two writers race hardest.

Reentrant within a thread, because an operation composed of other
operations on the same store is ordinary and deadlocking on itself would be
a worse failure than the one being fixed. Blocking rather than timing out,
because a writer that waits a millisecond is doing the right thing and a
writer that gives up loses the record this exists to protect.

Windows and POSIX both, via msvcrt and fcntl -- Windows because that is
where Dispatch runs, POSIX because that is where the suite runs.

After: twelve of twelve, repeatably across four runs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PohbRGCKjuPQQ9ZxJhJv83
